---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
---
<MotionPage slug="limits" title="Motion: limits" description="The current limits of @udixio/motion, family by family, as the package README states them.">
  <p>
    The package is under construction. Its current limits are listed here, family by family, each with what you can do about it. The
    page of a family repeats the limits that concern its code, next to that code. All seven verbs exist: <code>appear</code>,
    <code>disappear</code>, <code>emphasize</code>, <code>reveal</code>, <code>scrub</code>, <code>loop</code> and <code>layout</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Definitions and sequences</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If you need the values of a preset to be final, define your own motion: the presets are not calibrated yet.</li>
    <li>If you rely on a definition or a sequence being checked, check it in development: the validator runs in development only.</li>
    <li>If a cascade of a sequence step runs past <code>--udx-motion-stagger-max</code> (400 ms by default), its later delays are capped there. Give the late elements an <code>at</code> delay, which is not capped.</li>
    <li>If a child belongs to a nested sequence, the outer sequence does not count it as one of its steps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Presence, emphasis and reveal</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If a class of yours sets <code>opacity</code>, <code>translate</code>, <code>scale</code> or <code>rotate</code> on an element after its presence, emphasis or reveal has ended, the class has no effect: the end values that are not neutral stay inline until the binding is destroyed. Set those values with the motion instead.</li>
    <li>If a loop or a scrub moves an element, its animation overrides the same property while it runs. Do not animate that property with a class too.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Values and counters</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If you need a value's number at more than four decimals, compute it yourself: a value's number is rounded to four decimals.</li>
    <li>If you need the pointer outside the element, a pointer value cannot read it: listen to the pointer yourself.</li>
    <li>If the page scrolls inside an element rather than in the window, a scroll value follows the window's scroll, not that of the element.</li>
    <li>If you pause a time value, it resumes at the phase of its clock, not where it stopped.</li>
    <li>If you set <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on a time value, it holds the start of its range at once. Removing <code>none</code> runs it again at once.</li>
    <li>If a counter's element holds other text, that text is replaced once the counter moves: put the counter in an element of its own.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Gestures</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If you set <code>translate</code> on an element that a gesture binds, the gesture overwrites it while it is bound: move the element with a wrapper instead. <code>destroy()</code> gives back what the gesture wrote.</li>
    <li>If you set <code>touch-action</code> on an element that a gesture binds, the gesture sets its own value when it binds: <code>none</code> for a free drag, <code>pan-y</code> on the x axis, <code>pan-x</code> on the y axis. <code>destroy()</code> gives the value back.</li>
    <li>If you need a keyboard nudge of a drag to glide, it cannot: the element moves at once. A keyboard step of a reorder glides.</li>
    <li>A drag stays in the content box of the parent of its element, measured when the pointer grabs the element and at each key nudge.</li>
    <li>If an element must be reordered, it needs a parent: an element with no parent cannot be reordered, and development reports it.</li>
    <li>If a swipe is not prevented, the element stays in the DOM with <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>: remove it yourself when you no longer need it.</li>
    <li>If a gesture's spec is invalid, development reports it, and a production build trusts the spec. Its name is always checked.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Page transitions and text</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If the browser has no View Transitions API, <code>navigate()</code> runs the update alone, and no transition plays.</li>
    <li>If you use Astro's client router, <code>connectAstroTransitions()</code> starts no transition: it sets the direction of travel on <code>html</code>, and the router runs the transition.</li>
    <li>If a framework adds or removes the children of a split element, the motion does not follow them. Its text changes are followed, its elements are not: keep the children fixed, and change the text.</li>
    <li>If you split a text by lines and it holds an element other than <code>br</code>, the split falls back to words.</li>
    <li>If your text is in a joining script, such as Arabic, Indic or Thai, a split by characters splits it by words, so that no letter loses its form.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Scroll and scopes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If the visitor scrolls while <code>scrollTo</code> runs, the scroll stops where it is: start it again when you need it.</li>
    <li>If the browser has no view timeline, a scroll link does not follow the scroll: the element keeps its stylesheet state. See <a href="/motion/scrub">Link to the scroll</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Layout and shared elements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If a resizing element holds more than 24 elements, or nests them more than three levels deep, its content does not keep its natural size during the move.</li>
    <li>If a resizing element holds text directly, that text keeps its final size, and its start edge is interpolated. Text with no element around it is stretched during the move: wrap it in an element.</li>
    <li>If you change the <code>transform-origin</code> of a moving element, the move is wrong: keep it at the centre, where the move scales the element.</li>
    <li>If a move is caused by something outside the layout scope, it is not animated: keep the change inside the scope.</li>
    <li>If an element is bound more than 600 ms after the other holder of its shared identity went away, for example an island that hydrates late, it does not morph.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tokens and the manifest</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If you need final distances or staggers, calibrate them: the tokens are provisional, with low confidence, and they are not from M3.</li>
    <li>If you use a distance or a stagger token, suppress its translation or its stagger under reduced motion yourself: the tokens alone do not implement the policy.</li>
    <li>If you build on the manifest or the validator, expect changes: both are experimental. The manifest is published as <a href="/motion-manifest.json">motion-manifest.json</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
